the store @AliYar-Khan describes already exists as a separate process rather than a plugin. deja indexes the session files the agents already write — `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db`, `~/.claude/projects`, `~/.codex/sessions` and about thirty more — into its own sqlite under `~/.cache/deja`, and serves it over MCP. it survives reinstalling or deleting any of them because it isn't inside them, and it answers about sessions from before it was installed. disclosure: i maintain it — https://github.com/vshulcz/deja-vu

@robertn702 one thing that bites anything reading opencode's database directly: 2.0 renamed the tables. `session` is `session_v2`, and `message`/`part` are folded into one `session_message` table with the role in a `type` column and an assistant turn's parts inside its `data` blob. inside a turn, `bash` is `shell`, `apply_patch` is `patch`, the file a read or edit names is `path` rather than `filePath`, and what a tool printed moved from the `state.output` string to the `state.content` block list. i only found all of that by installing 2.0.12 and reading what it wrote — a user reported the store as unreadable first.

@RemanenetSpy on state invalidation: agreed about flat files, they do keep the old pattern and the new one side by side with nothing to separate them. transcripts aren't flat, though — a hit carries its session, its date and the line it came from, so the later decision outranks the earlier one and a reader can see there were two. and for "what actually worked" the useful record isn't a claim at all, it's the command that ran after the failing one and didn't fail again. that doesn't remove your problem, it just keeps the conflict visible instead of merging it into one asserted fact.
